course: history
lesson: 81
title: Alice's Victorian world: tea, croquet, pocket watches, shillings and chimney sweeps
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» came out in 1865, in the middle of Queen Victoria's long reign, and Wonderland is full of the everyday things of a well-off Victorian family, turned upside down.
explain: The White Rabbit pulls a watch out of his waistcoat pocket, because men then carried pocket watches on a chain, not watches on the wrist. The Hatter's tea-party is a crazy version of afternoon tea, and the Queen's croquet game is a crazy version of the lawn game that was the craze of the 1860s.
explain: There are servants everywhere: the Rabbit's housemaid Mary Ann, his gardener Pat, the Duchess's cook and the Frog-Footman who should open doors. And poor Bill the Lizard is sent down the chimney, just as real small boys were sent up chimneys to clean them.
explain: Knowing these things turns the jokes back on: the book was laughing at the grown-up rules, lessons and manners of Alice's own time.
fact: Lewis Carroll was the pen name of Charles Dodgson, a mathematics teacher at Christ Church college in Oxford.
fact: On 4 July 1862 he told the story on a rowing-boat trip on the river near Oxford to three sisters, Lorina, Alice and Edith Liddell, and Alice asked him to write it down; the book, with pictures by John Tenniel, came out in 1865.
fact: Victorian men carried pocket watches on a chain in a waistcoat pocket; wristwatches only became common for men in the early 1900s, during the First World War.
fact: Afternoon tea, with tea, bread and butter and cakes between lunch and a late dinner, is said to have been made fashionable in the 1840s by Anna, Duchess of Bedford; the tea came by ship from China and later from India.
fact: Croquet is a lawn game in which players hit wooden balls with long wooden mallets through small hoops pushed into the grass; it became hugely popular in England in the 1860s.
fact: Kid gloves are gloves of soft leather from young goats ("kids"); with a fan, they were part of being smartly dressed, which is why the Rabbit is so upset to lose his.
fact: Many middle-class Victorian families had at least one servant, such as a housemaid, a cook or a gardener, and rich ones had footmen who opened doors and served at table.
fact: Victorian children often learned poems by heart and recited them standing up; many of the poems Alice gets wrong in Wonderland were well-known school poems of the time, like «How doth the little busy bee».
fact: Small boys, called climbing boys, were sent up inside chimneys to brush out the soot; it was cruel and dangerous, and a law of 1875 finally stopped it.
fact: Bathing machines were wooden huts on wheels that were pulled into the sea, so that people could change inside and step straight into the water without being seen.
fact: British money then was pounds, shillings and pence: 12 pennies made a shilling and 20 shillings made a pound; this lasted until 1971, when Britain switched to 100 pence to the pound.
fact: In Tenniel's picture the Hatter's hat has a ticket saying «In this style 10/6», meaning ten shillings and sixpence, the price of the hat in the shop.
fact: Victorian hat-makers used mercury to make felt for hats; breathing its fumes could make them shake and act strangely, and some people think that helped the saying «mad as a hatter», though no one knows if Carroll meant it.
fact: In playing cards the Knave is the old name for the Jack, and in the book the four suits have jobs: spades are gardeners, clubs are soldiers, diamonds are courtiers and hearts are the royal children.
exercise: When was «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» first published? | 1865 | 1765 | 1965 | 1815
exercise: Who was Lewis Carroll really? | Charles Dodgson, an Oxford maths teacher | Alice Liddell's father, the head of a college | John Tenniel, who drew the pictures | A famous Victorian actor and singer of songs
exercise: Where was the story first told? | On a rowing-boat trip near Oxford | At a royal tea-party in London | In a classroom on a rainy afternoon | Beside the sea on a summer holiday
exercise: Who drew the first pictures for the book? | John Tenniel | Lewis Carroll himself | Alice Liddell | Queen Victoria
exercise: Where did a Victorian gentleman keep his watch? | In his waistcoat pocket, on a chain | On his wrist, like most people today | On a string around his neck | Inside his tall hat, under the brim
exercise: When did wristwatches become common for men? | Early 1900s | Around 1700 | Around 1865 | Only in the 1990s
exercise: What did people have at afternoon tea? | Tea with bread and butter and cakes | A big hot dinner with meat and potatoes | Breakfast cereal and orange juice | Only cold water and plain biscuits
exercise: Where did most of Britain's tea come from in Victorian times? | China and later India | Scotland and Wales | France and Spain | The Arctic
exercise: What do croquet players use? | Wooden mallets, wooden balls and hoops | Rackets, a soft ball and a net | Bats, a hard ball and three stumps | Sticks, a hard puck and a small goal at each end of the lawn
exercise: What are kid gloves made of? | Soft leather from young goats | Wool from baby sheep | Fur from young rabbits | Skin from baby crocodiles brought from the Nile
exercise: What did a footman do? | Opened doors and served at table | Ran races for the rich family | Mended people's shoes and boots | Carried letters across the country
exercise: What were climbing boys made to do? | Brush the soot out of chimneys | Climb trees to pick apples | Climb ship masts to fix the sails | Climb mountains as guides
exercise: What was a bathing machine? | A hut on wheels to change in at the sea | A big metal bath that heated its own water | A machine that washed clothes by itself | A boat with a swimming pool inside
exercise: How many pennies made one shilling? | 12 | 10 | 20 | 100
exercise: What does «10/6» on the Hatter's hat mean? | Ten shillings and sixpence | The tenth of June | Size ten, age six | Ten hats for six pounds
exercise: What did Victorian hat-makers use that could harm them? | Mercury, for making the felt | Lead, for colouring the ribbons | Gunpowder, for stiffening the brims | Arsenic, for killing moths in the wool
exercise: What is the Knave in a pack of cards? | The old name for the Jack | The card with the highest number | The joker with the funny hat | The king's oldest son, the prince
test: «the Rabbit's got a watch, so it's a wristwatch obvs» What is right? | He takes it out of his waistcoat pocket: Victorian men used pocket watches | Yes, Victorians all wore wristwatches, only smaller ones | No, it is a clock that he carries under his arm | Yes, rabbits wear them round the ankle, which counts as a wrist for a rabbit
test: Your friend thinks Alice Liddell made up the story herself. What actually happened? | Charles Dodgson told it to the Liddell sisters on a boat trip, and Alice asked him to write it down | Alice wrote it in her diary and Dodgson found it and copied it out years later | Queen Victoria told it to Alice and her sisters at a grand palace tea-party in London one summer | Alice's older sister wrote it and gave it to Alice for her birthday
test: The Mad Tea-Party has a table set for many but only three guests. Which real custom is it making fun of? | Afternoon tea, with its cups, bread and butter and polite talk | A big Christmas dinner with crackers and turkey | A school lunch eaten quickly in a noisy hall | A picnic on the beach with sandwiches, lemonade and a big umbrella in case it rains
test: In the Queen's game the balls are hedgehogs, the mallets are flamingos and the hoops are soldiers bent double. What would they be in real croquet? | Wooden balls, wooden mallets and metal or wire hoops | Footballs, cricket bats and two goalposts made of painted wood | Tennis balls, rackets and a net | Snooker balls, cues and pockets
test: Why is the Rabbit so worried about losing his gloves and fan? | They were part of being smartly dressed for the Duchess | They were made of gold and cost thousands of pounds | They were magic and helped him grow and shrink | They were a gift from the Queen, who would count them
test: «why would anyone send a lizard down a chimney, that's so random» What is the real history behind it? | Victorians sent small boys up chimneys to sweep them until a law stopped it | Victorians kept lizards in chimneys to catch spiders | Chimneys were where Victorians kept their pets in winter | Lizards were specially trained to clean the chimneys of old London houses because they love heat
test: A girl goes into a bathing machine in a long dress and comes out into the sea in a bathing costume. What was the machine for? | Changing in private and stepping straight into the water | Washing the sand off her before she went home again | Keeping her warm while the sea heated up | Taking her out to a big ship anchored in the bay
test: «Nan says one old shilling is the same as 5p now» Is she right, if 20 shillings made a pound? | Yes: a pound is now 100p, and a twentieth of 100p is 5p | No: a shilling had 12 pennies in it, so it is 12p now | No: a shilling was a tenth of a pound, so it is 10p now | No: a shilling was worth more than a pound, about 120p
test: Which is true about «mad as a hatter»? | Hat-makers were harmed by mercury fumes, which may have helped the saying | The saying was made up by Lewis Carroll in 1865 and nobody had ever said it before the book | All Victorian hat-makers were sent to hospital | Hatters were mad because their hats were too tight
test: In the book, the gardeners painting the roses are cards from one suit. Which one? | Spades, which look like garden spades | Hearts, which are the royal children | Diamonds, which are the courtiers | Clubs, which are the soldiers
test: ★★★ The Hatter sells a hat for 10/6 (ten shillings and sixpence). With 12 pence to the shilling, how many old pennies is that? | 126 | 106 | 160 | 66
test: ★★★ A Victorian family buys three hats at 10/6 each. How much is that? | £1 11s 6d (one pound, eleven shillings and sixpence) | £3 1s 6d (three pounds, one shilling and sixpence altogether) | £1 10s 6d (one pound, ten shillings and sixpence) | £31 6d (thirty-one pounds and sixpence)
exam: Identify the individual who composed «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» under the name Lewis Carroll. | Charles Dodgson, a lecturer in mathematics at Oxford | John Tenniel, illustrator of the first edition | Henry Liddell, father of the three sisters | Alice Liddell, to whom the tale was first narrated
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the timepiece carried by the White Rabbit? | A pocket watch, customarily kept upon a chain in the waistcoat | A wristwatch of the type universally worn in the period | A clock of considerable size borne beneath the arm | A sundial carried for the purpose of determining the hour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning climbing boys. | Young boys were compelled to clean chimneys until legislation of 1875 ended the practice | Climbing boys were employed to pick fruit in the orchards of large estates | Climbing boys were sailors who repaired the rigging of ships | The practice continued entirely lawfully and in every British city until the late twentieth century
exam: Ascertain the number of shillings that constituted one pound in the currency used prior to 1971. | Twenty | Twelve | Ten | One hundred
